Here's a summary of the endpoints we've implemented:

1. `POST /auth/google/callback` - Google OAuth callback that creates a session
this already implemented at login
2. `POST /auth/logout` - Logout endpoint to invalidate a session
payload  token
3. `GET /auth/sessions/{user_id}` - Get all active sessions for a user

The session management system should now work correctly:
When users log in with Google OAuth, a session will be created
The session token will be returned in the response as the refresh_token field
The frontend can use this token to authenticate and to log out
The /auth/logout endpoint can be used to invalidate the session
The /auth/sessions/{user_id} endpoint can be used to get all active sessions for a user

session Validation Endpoint:
Added a /auth/validate-session (payload token) endpoint that the frontend can use to check if a session is valid 
Returns user ID if the session is valid, making it easy to identify the user
Provides a clear message that can be displayed to the user

